problems.problem:
Problem 3. Identification of three organic sulfur compounds

The molecules of compounds $\mathbf{X}$, $\mathbf{Y}$ and $\mathbf{Z}$ each contain one sulfur atom, in each compound in a different, correspondingly higher oxidation state. Compound $\mathbf{X}$ consists of carbon, hydrogen and sulfur; compounds $\mathbf{Y}$ and $\mathbf{Z}$ also contain oxygen. The sulfur content of compounds $\mathbf{X}$, $\mathbf{Y}$ and $\mathbf{Z}$ is, respectively: $51.6\,\%$, $41.0\,\%$ and $34.0\,\%$. It is also known that none of these compounds forms sodium salts with aqueous $\ce{NaOH}$ solution, nor do they undergo hydrolysis.

Give:

a) the molar masses of compounds $\mathbf{X}$, $\mathbf{Y}$ and $\mathbf{Z}$;

b) the structural formulae of these compounds;

c) the structural formulae of stable isomers of these compounds, one for each (respectively $\mathbf{X}_1$, $\mathbf{Y}_1$ and $\mathbf{Z}_1$), which can form sodium salts with aqueous $\ce{NaOH}$ solution but do not undergo hydrolysis;

d) the structural formulae of one stable isomer of compound $\mathbf{Y}$ (of formula $\mathbf{Y}_2$) and one stable isomer of compound $\mathbf{Z}$ (of formula $\mathbf{Z}_2$) which undergo hydrolysis; write the equations of the hydrolysis of compounds $\mathbf{Y}_2$ and $\mathbf{Z}_2$ in a basic medium.

Atomic masses in [g/mol]: H – 1, C – 12, O – 16, S – 32.

