problems.problem:
Problem 2. An optically inactive disaccharide

Hydrolysis of the non-reducing, optically inactive disaccharide $\mathbf{I}$ gives two aldohexoses $\mathbf{II}$ and $\mathbf{III}$, which show the same physicochemical properties except for exactly opposite values of the specific rotation. After hydrolysis of the fully methylated disaccharide two 2,3,4,6-tetra-O-methyl derivatives $\mathbf{IV}$ and $\mathbf{V}$ are obtained, which again differ from each other only in the value of the optical rotation (values equal in magnitude, opposite in sign). Reduction of $\mathbf{II}$ and $\mathbf{III}$ gives the same alditol $\mathbf{VI}$ (hexane-1,2,3,4,5,6-hexaol), which is optically inactive (meso form). Compound $\mathbf{II}$, subjected to a chain-shortening reaction, gives an aldopentose of the D series, from which reduction gives the optically active alditol $\mathbf{VII}$ (pentane-1,2,3,4,5-pentaol). Knowing that in the disaccharide the configurations at both anomeric carbon atoms are of the $\alpha$ type, give:

a) the Fischer open-chain formulae of the aldohexoses $\mathbf{II}$ and $\mathbf{III}$;

b) the Fischer open-chain formula of the alditol $\mathbf{VI}$;

c) the Fischer open-chain formula of the alditol $\mathbf{VII}$;

d) the Haworth perspective formulae of the hydrolysis products $\mathbf{IV}$ and $\mathbf{V}$ of the methylated disaccharide (as $\alpha$ anomers);

e) the Haworth perspective formula of the disaccharide $\mathbf{I}$.

