problems.problem:
Problem 4. Organic nitrogen compounds

Compounds $\mathbf{A}$, $\mathbf{B}$, $\mathbf{C}$ and $\mathbf{D}$ are isomers for which the molecular ion peak in the mass spectra has the value $m/e = 123$.

Compound $\mathbf{A}$, a liquid with the smell of almonds, can be obtained by the reaction of benzene with a mixture of nitric(V) acid and concentrated sulfuric(VI) acid. Compound $\mathbf{B}$ is formed in the oxidation of the well-known poison nicotine.

Compound $\mathbf{C}$ reacts with iron(III) chloride forming a coloured complex, and compound $\mathbf{D}$ reduces Tollens' reagent and reacts, among others, with hydroxylamine ($\ce{NH2OH}$).

The molecules of compounds $\mathbf{A}$, $\mathbf{C}$ and $\mathbf{D}$ contain nitrogen–oxygen bonds.

In the (low-resolution) $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra obtained for compounds $\mathbf{C}$ and $\mathbf{D}$, in the range $\delta = 6.5 - 9.5\,\mathrm{ppm}$ there is a symmetrical pattern of two doublets, whereas for compounds $\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{B}$ a more complicated (unsymmetrical) pattern of signals is observed in this range. For compound $\mathbf{B}$ one singlet can be distinguished among these signals.

Give:

a. the structural formulae of compounds $\mathbf{A}$, $\mathbf{B}$, $\mathbf{C}$ and $\mathbf{D}$;

b. justifications of the structures derived, based on the information given in the text of the problem;

